  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn elongated area of high pressure extends from the Tennessee\nValley northeast towards the Northeastern US and Atlantic \nCanada. This large area of high pressure is allowing for \ncompletely clear skies over eastern New York and western New \nEngland with light southerly winds. Into the overnight hours, \nclear skies and light/calm winds will stay in place, allowing \nfor good radiational cooling once again. Although it won't be \nquite as cool as this morning, lows will generally range from \nthe upper 20s to mid 30s, with widespread frost formation once \nagain. Some patchy fog cannot be totally ruled out in some \nsheltered valleys near bodies of water, but fog will likely be \nfairly isolated due to the cold and dry airmass in place and \nsome low-level moisture contribution will deposit as frost as \nopposed to help developing fog. \n\nDuring the upcoming week, dry weather looks to remain in place\nacross the entire region through the week. A Nor'Easter\ndeveloping off the mid Atlantic coast is expected to slowly sit\nand spin near the Carolinas and Delmarva, but will likely be too\nfar south to have a direct impact on our weather. Surface high\npressure and upper level ridging look to stay in place over the\nNortheast through the week. Most operational forecast guidance \n(including AI models) and ensembles keep any precip south of the\nregion closer to the mid Atlantic coast. If the storm tracks \nclose enough, some breezy conditions are possible across the mid\nHudson Valley and NW CT, but the overall consensus at this time\nis for winds to stay fairly light across most of the region \nthrough the week. Daytime temps are expected to be near seasonal\nnormals with upper 50s to low 60s in valley areas. With good \nradiational cooling, it will drop into the 30s each night.",
